Q: Bulbs for cs50x (third and fourth bit always 1, no matter the input) A: <p>Analyzing this issue along with the good comments, I decided to extrapolate the issue and comments into refactored code that reflected the desired outcome.</p>
<p>First off, though, compiling the original code caused my compiler to complain about the definition of the &quot;BITS_IN_BYTES&quot; declaration and subsequent use in declaring the &quot;array&quot; integer array.</p>
<pre><code>const int BITS_IN_BYTE = 8;
</code></pre>
<pre><code>int array[BITS_IN_BYTE] = {0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0};
</code></pre>
<pre><code>/home/craig/C_Programs/Console/Bulbs/main.c|14|error: variable-sized object may not be initialized|
</code></pre>
<p>The quickest solution (but possibly not the optimal solution) was to refactor that line of code using a &quot;#define&quot; statement.</p>
<pre><code>#define BITS_IN_BYTE 8
</code></pre>
<p>With that in mind, and again reviewing all of the comments going back and forth, following is a refactored version of the code providing the interpretation of characters into their ASCII code values, and subsequently deriving their 8-bit representation with &quot;on/off&quot; bulbs.</p>
<pre><code>#define BITS_IN_BYTE 8

void print_bulb(int);

int main(void)
{
    string text = get_string(&quot;Text to encrypt!: &quot;);
    int n = strlen(text), array[BITS_IN_BYTE] = {0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0};

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n ; i++)
    {
        for (int x = BITS_IN_BYTE - 1; x &gt;= 0; x--)
        {
            array[x] = text[i] % 2;
            text[i]  = text[i] / 2; //jak dojdzie do 1 to?
        }

        for (int y = 0; y &lt; BITS_IN_BYTE; y++)
        {
            print_bulb(array[y]);
        }
        printf(&quot;\n&quot;);
    }
}

void print_bulb(int bit)
{
    if (bit == 0)
    {
        printf(&quot;\U000026AB&quot;);   /* Dark emoji                   */
    }
    else if (bit == 1)
    {
        printf(&quot;\U0001F7E1&quot;);   /* Light emoji                  */
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In testing this out utilizing examples from the CS50 course, following are a couple of tests with matching inputs and resulting bulb displays.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Bulbs/bin/Release$ ./Bulbs 
Text to encrypt!: HI!
⚫🟡⚫⚫🟡⚫⚫⚫
⚫🟡⚫⚫🟡⚫⚫🟡
⚫⚫🟡⚫⚫⚫⚫🟡
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Bulbs/bin/Release$ ./Bulbs 
Text to encrypt!: HI MOM
⚫🟡⚫⚫🟡⚫⚫⚫
⚫🟡⚫⚫🟡⚫⚫🟡
⚫⚫🟡⚫⚫⚫⚫⚫
⚫🟡⚫⚫🟡🟡⚫🟡
⚫🟡⚫⚫🟡🟡🟡🟡
⚫🟡⚫⚫🟡🟡⚫🟡
</code></pre>
<p>Also, testing out a digit scenario as noted in the issue, following is an additional test with just digits.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Bulbs/bin/Release$ ./Bulbs 
Text to encrypt!: 54321
⚫⚫🟡🟡⚫🟡⚫🟡
⚫⚫🟡🟡⚫🟡⚫⚫
⚫⚫🟡🟡⚫⚫🟡🟡
⚫⚫🟡🟡⚫⚫🟡⚫
⚫⚫🟡🟡⚫⚫⚫🟡
</code></pre>
<p>If one parses each &quot;bulb set&quot; against the binary representation value in the ASCII table, each character matches.</p>
<p>Probably the conclusion here is more study of the ASCII table would be in order as it pertains to each character's value as an integer value, hexadecimal value, and binary value.</p>

Q: API key in header with swashbuckle A: <p>After a bunch of hitting my head against the wall, here's the solution I ended up with:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen(
    c=&gt; {
        c.AddSecurityDefinition(&quot;ApiKeyHeader&quot;, new OpenApiSecurityScheme
        {
            In = ParameterLocation.Header,
            Name = &quot;x-api-key&quot;,
            Type = SecuritySchemeType.ApiKey,
            Description = &quot;Authorization by x-api-key inside request's header&quot;
        });
        c.AddSecurityRequirement(doc =&gt; new OpenApiSecurityRequirement
        {
            {
                new OpenApiSecuritySchemeReference(&quot;ApiKeyHeader&quot;, doc),
                new List&lt;string&gt;()
            }
        });
        c.SwaggerDoc(&quot;v1&quot;, new Info { Title = &quot;[anything]&quot;, Version = &quot;v1&quot; });
    });
</code></pre>
